    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 10px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 37.5%;"><b>Theme:</b> She Is My Mom!</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Recognize when written, without visual support, not only their own name but also the names of classmates or family members.
Reading: Point to simple words in the text that identify objects or characters in a picture book.
Phonemic Awareness: Identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can recognize family-related words, including own name, in written texts (e.g., "mom," "dad").
Can match names of family members to pictures in books (e.g., matching the word "father" to a picture of a father in a family picture book).
Can identify sounds of the letters of the alphabet when supported with a concrete item or picture representing that sound (e.g., identifying the sound of the letter "S" in the word "sister" when shown a picture of a sister).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the focus for the lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, plays a familiar "Hello" song. Then, the teacher holds up large flashcards of family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather) from Lesson 1, saying the names clearly. Students chorally repeat the names. The teacher asks students to point to their own family members if they have a family photo or picture on their desk (realia or simplified drawings). This connects to the theme "She Is My Mom!" and activates previously learned vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of "mom" with the word "mom" clearly written below the picture. The teacher points to the word "mom" and says, "This is 'mom'." The teacher then points to the picture and says, "She is my mom." The teacher repeats this with "dad" and "sister," emphasizing the written word and its corresponding image and sound. The teacher models the pronunciation of /m/ in "mom" and /d/ in "dad" by saying the words slowly and clearly, and having students repeat chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions and gestures:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the written word "mom" on a flashcard): "Is this 'mom'?" (Students respond with "Yes!" or thumbs up).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a picture of a brother and showing the word "dad"): "Is this 'dad'?" (Students respond with "No!" or thumbs down).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing the written word "sister"): "What sound does this word start with? /s/ or /m/?" (Teacher models the sounds, students respond with "S!").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I". Focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a large, colorful picture book or a big print-out of a short, simple story (a narrative excerpt) about a family, featuring clear illustrations. The text uses target vocabulary: "This is my family. She is my mom. He is my dad. This is my brother. This is my sister." Each sentence is short and accompanied by a picture of the family member being described. The names of the family members (e.g., "mom," "dad") are written in large, clear font.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing Family Roles: Using appropriate language to describe family members and their roles (e.g., "This is my mother." / "She is my mom.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud slowly, pointing to each word as it is read. Students follow along. Then the teacher performs the following:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this story about?" (Teacher points to the pictures and gestures 'family'). Students can respond by pointing to a family picture.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher reads a sentence, for example, "She is my mom," and asks, "Where is the mom?" Students point to the picture of the mom in the book/print-out. The teacher then asks, "Where is the word 'mom'?" and students point to the written word in the text. This is repeated for "dad," "brother," and "sister."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> The teacher points to the word "brother" in the text and asks, "What sound do you hear at the beginning of 'brother'?" (Teacher models /b/). Students respond chorally or by gesturing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 12 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading. Reinforcing recognition of family words and their sounds.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Personal pronouns + verb ("She is," "He is"), Possessive adjectives ("my mom").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Identifying sounds /m/ in "mom," /d/ in "dad," /b/ in "brother," /s/ in "sister."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Word-Picture Matching Worksheet (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of different family members on one side and the written words ("mom," "dad," "brother," "sister") on the other side, scrambled. Students draw a line to match each picture to its correct word. The teacher monitors and helps with pronunciation, asking, "Who is this?" or "Find 'mom'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "Find the Word" Game (Semi-controlled Drill):</b> The teacher writes the words "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister" on the whiteboard in a mixed order. The teacher calls out a word (e.g., "Find 'sister'!") and students point to the correct word on the board. The teacher then asks, "What sound is at the beginning of 'sister'?" (Students say /s/). This is repeated for all target words, reinforcing both visual recognition and initial sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Family Word Flashcard Recognition (Pair Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a small set of flashcards with just the words "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "grandmother," "grandfather." One student holds up a word card, and the other student reads it aloud and points to a corresponding picture in a small picture book (provided by the teacher) or draws a quick stick figure. They take turns reading and identifying.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 13 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I". Focus on reading recognition and collaboration.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "Family Word Detective" Activity (Small Group Collaboration)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher prepares a large poster or a "mini-book" for each group, which features simple pictures of a family and short, mixed-up sentences or individual words from the theme "She Is My Mom!" (e.g., "mom," "He is dad," "my sister," "She is grandmother"). The task is for students to "be detectives" and circle all the family words they can find ("mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," "grandmother," "grandfather"). They also practice pointing to the words and saying them to their group members, encouraging peer support and recognition.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing groups, and prompting: "Can you find 'dad'?" or "What sound does 'brother' start with?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For an extension, students can point to a word and then point to the corresponding family member in a group photo or drawing they brought from home (if applicable and prepared in advance).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check student learning and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist and individual worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher gives each student a simple worksheet. The worksheet has 3-4 pictures of family members (e.g., mom, dad, sister) and a list of the corresponding words in a different order below the pictures. Students are instructed to draw a line from each picture to its correct word. Additionally, students are asked to circle the first letter of the word "sister" and "brother" on the worksheet to check phonemic awareness.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes family-related words (e.g., "mom," "dad," "sister") in written texts (matches words to pictures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies the correct initial letter sounds of target words (e.g., /s/ for "sister," /b/ for "brother").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two simple prompts to respond to by drawing a happy face or a sad face, or a thumbs up/down:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the word 'mom'!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I know the sound of 'S' in 'sister'!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 8 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 5px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection on the lesson and connection to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b> The teacher briefly reviews the exit tickets and asks students to think about:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new family word can you read by yourself now?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading about family was fun today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well today? (e.g., The word-picture matching activity was highly engaging and effective for word recognition. Students responded well to the "Find the Word" game for phonemic awareness.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Some students still struggle with 'brother' and 'sister' due to similar length; perhaps incorporate more distinct visual cues or a phonics song for these specific words. Need to ensure the reading materials are always clearly linked to large, simple pictures.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we read about family. Next time, we will talk about our families and introduce them to our friends using the words we learned today! We will practice saying 'She is my mom' and 'He is my dad' to each other." (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>